From 3d127b3b56515b201cef758d1e8e8d6f4030370c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fbalazsy <frantisekbalazsy@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:29:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 08/11] drm/apple: fabric: hand the hybrid between an HDMI and
 a two-pipe Type-C display

A monitor on both the HDMI port and a USB-C port of a J616s (a Samsung
G95NC at 7680x2160) can have both display pipes on one input only: the
hybrid dcpext0 is the HDMI output's pipeline and the only primary of a
pair.  The monitor does not tell which input it shows: both keep HPD
up, and the unselected DP input still answers for its EDID.  So
typec_hybrid_first says who gets the hybrid: -1 follows the device tree
(the default), 0 puts an HDMI display first, 1 a Type-C display.  A
write applies at once, so that the desktop can follow the monitor's
input.

 - Type-C first: a Type-C display that asked for two pipes and found no
   peer takes the hybrid.  The HDMI display is reported gone, its link
   released (retried while the firmware is busy with a link nobody
   answers) and it waits; it gets the hybrid back when the Type-C
   display sets a mode within one pipe (picture by picture) or leaves.
 - HDMI first: an HDMI display keeps the hybrid.  One wider than one
   pipe also takes the other pipe from a Type-C display, which waits,
   routed nowhere, until the HDMI display fits one pipe or leaves.

A hand-over is an unplug and replug: at most one per ten seconds, and
no more than two failed takes until the next plug.

Peer reservations that outlived their pair are dropped: when a Type-C
route takes a pipeline reserved as another display's peer, and when a
display asks for a pair while the pipe it reserved, or the pipe it is
reserved for, drives a display of its own.
